5  isotropy: a spherically symmetric well distribution
==============================================================================
   Read literally the requirement is 'S -> 0'.  That is true AT THE
   CENTRE and false anywhere else, and the difference matters, so
   both are measured.

   (a) 1440 wells in an exact octahedral orbit, field point at the centre
       plaw      |S| at the centre 4.952e-17   |K - exp(s0) I|_max 4.441e-16
       expo      |S| at the centre 5.910e-17   |K - exp(s0) I|_max 8.882e-16
       gscreen   |S| at the centre 4.150e-17   |K - exp(s0) I|_max 4.441e-16

   (b) 2e+06 wells uniform in a 1.5 Mpc ball, probe on the x axis
       The continuum column is a 2-D Gauss-Legendre quadrature of
       the same integral -- the exact answer the Monte-Carlo must
       reproduce.  Monte-Carlo noise on S_rr is ~1/sqrt(N) = 7.1e-04.

       r (kpc)   S_rr (MC)   S_rr (exact)    difference    form residual
             0    -0.000432    +0.000000     4.32e-04      0.6674
           100    -0.000133    +0.000016     1.49e-04      0.9768
           300    +0.000460    +0.000159     3.01e-04      0.9413
           600    +0.001638    +0.000844     7.94e-04      0.5942
           900    +0.003610    +0.003320     2.90e-04      0.1901
          1200    +0.017115    +0.016479     6.36e-04      0.0436
          2500    +0.515068    +0.515045     2.28e-05      0.0002
   [PASS] 5 S = 0 at the centre; off-centre S matches the exact spherical form
          exact octahedral configuration, |S| at the centre: 5.910e-17 (round-off)
          K there equals exp(s_0) I to 4.4e-16 -- an isotropic rescaling, no preferred direction
          random ball, |S_rr| at the centre 4.32e-04 against Monte-Carlo noise 7.1e-04
          largest |MC - exact quadrature| over all probe radii: 7.94e-04 (1.1 sigma_MC)
          OFF-CENTRE S IS NOT ZERO AND MUST NOT BE: at 1200 kpc inside the ball S_rr = +0.0171, at 2500 kpc outside it +0.5151.
          Reading 'S -> 0 for a spherical distribution' as a statement about every
          point is wrong -- the direction field n_a(x) seen from an off-centre point
          is not isotropic.  What symmetry requires is the form lambda(r)(rhat rhat
          - I/3), and the largest residual from that form, over the probes where |S|
          rises above the Monte-Carlo floor, is 0.0436.

